[patterns/application/micro-frontend/PTN-APP-MFE-011-shared-context-pattern.md#variation-3-multiple-independent-contexts]

#### Variation 3: Multiple Independent Contexts

Split large monolithic context into multiple fine-grained contexts.

**When to Use:**
- Prevent unnecessary re-renders (NotificationContext changes don't re-render prior auth workflow consumers)
- Different ownership (UserContext owned by Auth team, PriorAuthWorkflowContext owned by Utilization Management team)
- Different lifecycle (SessionContext persists, ModalContext ephemeral)

**Implementation:**

```typescript
// shell/src/App.tsx
export function App() {
  return (
    <UserProvider>       {/* User profile, permissions */}
      <NotificationProvider>  {/* Notifications */}
        <PriorAuthWorkflowProvider>  {/* Prior authorization workflow state */}
          <ThemeProvider>        {/* Theme, dark mode */}
            <BrowserRouter>
              {/* Routes and modules */}
            </BrowserRouter>
          </ThemeProvider>
        </PriorAuthWorkflowProvider>
      </NotificationProvider>
    </UserProvider>
  );
}

// Modules consume only the contexts they need
function HeaderBadge() {
  const { count } = useNotifications();  // Only subscribes to NotificationContext
  return <Badge count={count} />;
}
```

**Benefits:**
- **Performance**: Components only re-render when their subscribed context changes
- **Modularity**: Contexts can be added/removed independently
- **Ownership**: Different teams own different contexts

**Considerations:**
- **Context Nesting**: Deep nesting of 10+ contexts reduces readability
- **Provider Composition**: Use custom `AppProviders` component to compose all providers

