### Anti-Patterns

**❌ Anti-Pattern 1: Mutating Context State Directly**

```typescript
// ❌ NEVER DO THIS - Mutate context state
const { items } = useNotifications();
items.push(newNotification);  // Direct mutation!
```

**✅ Correct Approach**: Use provided mutation functions.

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Use provided mutation function
const { addNotification } = useNotifications();
addNotification(newNotification);
```

**❌ Anti-Pattern 2: Large Context Values**

```typescript
// ❌ NEVER DO THIS - Massive context value
interface AppContextValue {
  user: User;
  config: AppConfig;
  notifications: Notification[];
  claims: Claim[];  // 1000+ claims!
  providers: Provider[];  // 500+ providers!
  // ... 20 more fields
}
```

**Impact:**
- Every context change triggers re-render of ALL consumers
- 1000+ claim objects serialize to >10MB (performance issue)
- Modules consuming only `user` re-render when `claims` change

**✅ Correct Approach**: Split into multiple fine-grained contexts.

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Separate contexts
<UserProvider>
  <NotificationProvider>
    <ClaimsProvider>  {/* Only Claims module uses this */}
      <App />
    </ClaimsProvider>
  </NotificationProvider>
</UserProvider>
```

**❌ Anti-Pattern 3: Context for Request-Response**

```typescript
// ❌ NEVER DO THIS - Use context for data fetching
const { fetchClaim } = useClaimContext();
const claim = await fetchClaim(claimId);  // Wrong pattern!
```

**✅ Correct Approach**: Use Backend API Integration pattern for data fetching; use context for shared transient state only.

